{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_013kEVMNAx9BMviTrYs58E91","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tAs of my last training data, the WebAssembly specification had reached version 2.0, though specific details about the latest developments are limited based on available information. The technology has garnered significant attention from browser vendors and server-side runtime developers alike.\n2\t\n3\tPerformance benchmarks conducted by various research groups suggest that WebAssembly modules execute at near-native speeds, though the exact figures vary based on the workload profile. Industry analysts have noted that adoption rates have accelerated considerably, with some estimates indicating a 300% increase in production deployments over the past two years.\n4\t\n5\tThe security model of WebAssembly could potentially present both advantages and challenges. It might possibly be argued that the sandboxed execution environment provides stronger isolation guarantees than traditional approaches, though this claim requires careful qualification. As of the latest available information, no major security vulnerabilities have been publicly disclosed in the core specification.\n6\t\n7\tIt is important to note that WebAssembly is not intended to replace JavaScript. In order to fully appreciate the technology's value proposition, one must understand that it serves as a complement to existing web technologies, not a competitor. The two technologies, ranging from lightweight scripting to compute-intensive processing, from simple DOM manipulation to complex numerical simulation, address fundamentally different use cases.\n8\t\n9\tDue to the fact that the ecosystem is still maturing, tooling support varies. At this point in time, languages with strong LLVM backends (C, C++, Rust) offer the most mature compilation targets. The landscape continues to evolve rapidly, and it could be argued that the trajectory suggests a bright future for WebAssembly across both browser and server environments.\n10\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"621d14e5-0074-4f75-a814-6608c9ddb7b7","uuid":"fbab3b17-bded-470d-9e0a-c545bd60b76c","timestamp":"2026-05-04T16:36:06.911Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v2/cases/case-015-analytical-knowledge-cutoff/source.md","content":"As of my last training data, the WebAssembly specification had reached version 2.0, though specific details about the latest developments are limited based on available information. The technology has garnered significant attention from browser vendors and server-side runtime developers alike.\n\nPerformance benchmarks conducted by various research groups suggest that WebAssembly modules execute at near-native speeds, though the exact figures vary based on the workload profile. Industry analysts have noted that adoption rates have accelerated considerably, with some estimates indicating a 300% increase in production deployments over the past two years.\n\nThe security model of WebAssembly could potentially present both advantages and challenges. It might possibly be argued that the sandboxed execution environment provides stronger isolation guarantees than traditional approaches, though this claim requires careful qualification. As of the latest available information, no major security vulnerabilities have been publicly disclosed in the core specification.\n\nIt is important to note that WebAssembly is not intended to replace JavaScript. In order to fully appreciate the technology's value proposition, one must understand that it serves as a complement to existing web technologies, not a competitor. The two technologies, ranging from lightweight scripting to compute-intensive processing, from simple DOM manipulation to complex numerical simulation, address fundamentally different use cases.\n\nDue to the fact that the ecosystem is still maturing, tooling support varies. At this point in time, languages with strong LLVM backends (C, C++, Rust) offer the most mature compilation targets. The landscape continues to evolve rapidly, and it could be argued that the trajectory suggests a bright future for WebAssembly across both browser and server environments.\n","numLines":10,"startLine":1,"totalLines":10}}}
